---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/phlebotomy/foundational/veins-and-blood-the-phlebotomist-s-map">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an early lesson in Phlebotomy and it assumes no prior anatomy or laboratory
    training: every vessel, additive and procedure step is defined here, and the whole map is
    built from one bend of the elbow and one tube of blood. What you need is the understanding
    that a five-minute procedure can carry a patient's whole diagnosis.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>name the three vessel types and say why veins — and which arm veins — are the draw sites;</li>
    <li>describe what blood is made of, and why the tube colours are blood chemistry;</li>
    <li>give the vein preference order and the sites that are never used, with the harm each exclusion prevents;</li>
    <li>follow the procedure step by step and state the safety logic of each step;</li>
    <li>explain the order of draw as chemistry law rather than bureaucracy.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Phlebotomy is the procedure that starts most diagnoses: the blood draw. A majority of
    clinical decisions lean on laboratory results, and nearly every one of those results
    begins with a phlebotomist's five minutes of work — which is why the procedure looks
    simple and is not. Compressed into those minutes are anatomy (which vein, and which
    structures the needle must miss), chemistry (which tube for which test, in which order)
    and trust (a needle, a stranger, a steady hand).
  </p>
  <p>
    The foundational unit builds the map the whole profession works from. It starts with the
    circulatory system's three vessel types and why veins — specifically certain veins of
    the arm — are the draw sites; it continues with what blood actually is, because the
    tubes' additives and the order they are drawn in are blood chemistry; and it finishes
    with the applied craft: site selection, the sites that are never used, and the procedure
    that wraps anatomy and chemistry into a safe draw. The learning goal is a specific kind
    of knowledge: the phlebotomist's map, consulted in real time, over a real arm, with a
    real patient watching.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from patient ID to bedside labelling" widget="equationStep" config={orderCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five steps are the whole procedure compressed — two
    spoken identifiers matched to the requisition (the single most safety-critical step), the
    vein preference order with its exclusions, the order of draw that prevents invisible
    additive carryover, the fill-and-mix mechanics, and labelling at the bedside in the
    patient's presence. Each step's safety logic gets its own section below.
  </p>

  <h2>Veins, arteries and capillaries</h2>
  <p>
    The circulatory system's three vessel types divide its labour. <strong>Arteries</strong>
    carry blood away from the heart under high pressure; they are deep, thick-walled,
    muscular and pulsatile — which is exactly why phlebotomy avoids them: a punctured
    artery bleeds vigorously, is harder to compress, and can complicate seriously.
    <strong>Capillaries</strong> are the microscopic exchange vessels — one cell thick,
    delivering oxygen and nutrients to tissue and collecting waste; capillary blood (from a
    fingerstick or heelstick) is the draw of last resort and the routine draw in newborns
    and point-of-care testing. <strong>Veins</strong> return blood to the heart under low
    pressure through thin, distensible walls — and they are the venipuncture target for
    three practical reasons: they lie <em>superficially</em> (many visible and palpable
    under the skin), they tolerate puncture and pressure well, and they refill after the
    needle leaves. Blood drawn from veins (venous blood) also differs measurably from
    arterial blood — lower oxygen, different pH — which is why the draw type must match
    the test.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="file each property under its vessel type" widget="sort-buckets" config={phlVesselSort} />
  <p>
    The phlebotomist's map is drawn on the antecubital fossa — the bend of the elbow —
    where three superficial veins form an "H" or "M" pattern. The <strong>median cubital
    vein</strong> is first choice almost always: usually the largest and best-anchored of
    the three, and — the anatomy that matters most — it sits <em>away from the median
    nerve and the brachial artery</em>, the two deep structures a careless needle can
    find. The <strong>cephalic vein</strong> runs along the thumb side of the forearm and
    is the second choice (often tender and prone to rolling). The <strong>basilic
    vein</strong>, on the little-finger side, is last choice among the three: it lies
    closest to the median nerve and brachial artery, and basilic draws carry the highest
    nerve-injury risk — used when it is clearly the best vein, with extra care. When arm
    veins fail, the back of the hand and wrist offer smaller, more mobile veins drawn with
    a smaller needle; ankle and leg veins are generally avoided (clot and dependency
    problems) and are a physician-decision territory. Anatomy here is not academic: it is
    the difference between a routine draw and an injury claim.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="build the vein preference order, first choice to refused" widget="order" config={phlVeinOrder} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a vein is wherever you see something blue. Veins
    <em>palpate</em>, they do not just look — a good draw site feels like a firm, springy
    tube that rebounds, and the skill is distinguishing vein from tendon (hard, and moves
    with finger motion) and from artery (pulsates). Sight misleads; touch decides.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair what each finding under the finger means" widget="match" config={phlPalpateMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> arteries carry blood away under high pressure and are avoided;
    capillaries are the one-cell exchange vessels, the draw of last resort except in newborns;
    and veins are the target because they lie superficially, tolerate puncture and refill. The
    antecubital map runs median cubital first, cephalic second, basilic last — and the vein is
    found by touch, not sight. Next: what the blood being drawn actually is.
  </p>

  <h2>Blood composition</h2>
  <p>
    Blood is a liquid tissue whose two fractions explain the phlebotomist's tubes. Spin a
    tube of blood and it separates: about 55 per cent <strong>plasma</strong> — a pale
    yellow fluid of water, proteins, electrolytes, nutrients, hormones and waste — and
    about 45 per cent <strong>formed elements</strong>: red blood cells (erythrocytes,
    the oxygen carriers, several million per microlitre), white blood cells (leukocytes,
    the immune defenders, around 4,000–11,000 per microlitre) and platelets (thrombocytes,
    the clotting fragments). The red cells' share is the <strong>haematocrit</strong> —
    around 45 per cent in men, 40 per cent in women — and it is why blood is viscous and
    why a "blood" test is really several different tests on several different fractions.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="guess the magnitude — cells in a microlitre" widget="estimate" config={phlWbcEstimate} />
  <p>
    Those fractions are what the tube colours select. <strong>Serum versus plasma</strong>
    is the central distinction: let a tube clot and centrifuge it, and the fluid above the
    clot is <em>serum</em> (plasma minus its clotting proteins — fibrinogen consumed);
    keep the tube from clotting with an anticoagulant, and the fluid is <em>plasma</em>
    (everything, clotting proteins included). Most chemistry runs on serum or plasma;
    haematology (the cell count) runs on whole blood preserved unclotted. The additives
    do the selecting: <strong>EDTA</strong> (lavender top) chelates calcium so blood
    cannot clot, preserving cells in their true proportions for the complete blood count;
    <strong>citrate</strong> (light blue) also binds calcium but reversibly, so the
    laboratory can re-add a known amount of calcium and watch the sample clot — which is
    how clotting tests (PT/INR, aPTT) work, and why citrate tubes must fill to their
    exact ratio (nine parts blood to one part citrate): a short fill changes the ratio
    and poisons the result. <strong>Heparin</strong> (green) anticoagulates for
    STAT chemistry; <strong>fluoride</strong> (grey) inhibits the glycolytic enzymes that
    would otherwise consume the sample's glucose hour by hour; <strong>clot activator
    and gel</strong> (red and gold) make serum. Every tube is a chemistry decision made
    in advance by the system — the phlebotomist's job is to draw the right ones, in the
    right order, filled correctly.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> serum and plasma are two names for the same fluid. They differ by exactly the clotting proteins: serum is what remains once the sample has clotted (fibrinogen consumed), plasma is the fluid of anticoagulated blood with those proteins still aboard. The distinction decides which tube each test requires — which is why a serum test collected in a citrate or EDTA tube is a rejected specimen, not a delayed one.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> blood separates into plasma (about 55 per cent) and formed
    elements (about 45 per cent — the haematocrit); serum is plasma minus its clotting
    proteins; and each tube colour is an additive making a chemistry decision in advance —
    EDTA for the cell count, citrate for clotting tests at an exact fill ratio, heparin for
    STAT chemistry, fluoride for glucose, clot activator for serum. Next: where on the body all
    of this may and may not happen.
  </p>

  <h2>Vein selection and sites to avoid</h2>
  <p>
    Site selection is anatomy applied, and it follows a preference order: median cubital
    first, cephalic second, basilic third, hand veins fourth. Then comes the exclusion
    map — the sites never used, each with a reason written in harm. The <strong>arm on the
    mastectomy side</strong>: lymph-node removal can leave that arm prone to
    lymphoedema and infection, and a puncture invites both — it is a lifelong exclusion.
    <strong>IV sites and cannulas</strong>: drawing below an infusion dilutes the sample
    with whatever is infusing; drawing above it risks withdrawing the medication.
    <strong>Haematomas, scars, burns, rashes and oedema</strong>: compromised tissue gives
    compromised samples (and oedematous tissue is a nerve-injury setting). <strong>Vascular
    access devices and fistulas</strong> (dialysis grafts and fistulas): absolutely never —
    they are the patient's lifeline. A mastectomy exclusion on one arm plus an IV on the
    other is the classic scenario where the phlebotomist must stop and consult rather
    than improvise.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="walk the two-arms problem" widget="branch" config={phlSitesBranch} />
  <p>
    Around the site selection runs the procedure the widget maps, and each step has its
    safety logic. Patient identification with two identifiers spoken by the patient — the
    single most safety-critical step, because every downstream error is downstream of a
    wrong tube's label. Tourniquet time under a minute (prolonged tightening
    <em>hemoconcentrates</em> the blood — cells concentrated, fluid filtered out — and
    skews the results). Tubes filled and gently inverted to mix their additives; the
    tourniquet released before the needle comes out; pressure applied after. And
    <strong>labelling at the bedside</strong>, in the patient's presence, never pre-printed
    or back at the desk: an unlabelled specimen means "start again", and the rule exists
    because specimen labelling errors are exactly how one patient's results end up in
    another patient's chart — with transfusions, in the worst case.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="true or false — the procedure's safety logic" widget="judge" config={phlProcJudge} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the order of draw is bureaucratic convention. It is
    chemistry law: the needle carries traces of each tube's additive into the next tube,
    so drawing out of order cross-contaminates samples invisibly — EDTA carryover (from
    lavender) binds the calcium of a chemistry tube and wrecks its potassium and calcium
    results; citrate carryover ruins clotting tests. The sequence (blood cultures first
    for sterility, then light blue, red/gold, green, lavender, grey) is protection against
    errors no one could see, in a field where no one can see anything until the result
    comes back.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="find the faulty step in this colleague's tube sequence" widget="spot-error" config={phlDrawSpot} />

  <h2>Capillary draws and special populations</h2>
  <p>
    When veins are not an option, the map moves to the capillaries. <strong>Fingersticks</strong>
    (adults and children) puncture the fleshy pad of the middle or ring finger with a
    lancet; <strong>heelsticks</strong> (newborns) puncture the outer edges of the heel,
    warming the site first to bring blood to the surface. Capillary technique has its own
    grammar: wipe away the first drop (tissue fluid dilutes it), collect free-flowing
    drops without squeezing hard (haemolysis and tissue fluid again), and fill
    microtainers in the same order of draw as venous draws. The results differ measurably —
    capillary blood runs slightly higher in haemoglobin and glucose and lower in some
    chemistry values than venous — so the laboratory's reference ranges must match the
    collection type. The special populations where capillary sampling dominates tell the
    story of why it exists: newborns (tiny total blood volume — a full venous draw would
    be a meaningful fraction of it), patients with inaccessible veins, and point-of-care
    testing at the bedside. Special populations reshape venipuncture too: paediatric
    positioning and comfort holds, geriatric fragile veins on low vacuum and small
    needles, and oncology patients whose veins have been consumed by treatment — each a
    reminder that the map is drawn for the person in the chair, not the average patient.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="drag the divider — venous draw vs capillary draw" widget="compare" config={phlCapCompare} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why is the median cubital vein first choice and the basilic vein last — and which structures make the basilic risky?</li>
    <li>What exactly differs between serum and plasma, and why is a serum test collected in a citrate or EDTA tube a rejected specimen rather than a delayed one?</li>
    <li>A colleague draws the lavender EDTA tube before the light-blue citrate tube. Which results are at risk, and what principle is the order of draw enforcing?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The median cubital is usually the largest and best-anchored
    of the three antecubital veins and sits away from the median nerve and the brachial artery;
    the basilic lies closest to both, so basilic draws carry the highest nerve-injury risk.
    (2) The clotting proteins: serum is what remains after the sample clots (fibrinogen
    consumed), plasma keeps them aboard; a serum test needs those proteins intact, and an
    additive tube has bound the sample's calcium and altered the specimen itself, so it cannot
    yield a valid result. (3) The clotting tests on the citrate tube — and the potassium and
    calcium of any later chemistry tube, because EDTA carryover binds calcium invisibly. The
    order of draw is protection against cross-contamination no one could see until the result
    comes back.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Phlebotomy starts most diagnoses: anatomy, chemistry and trust compressed into five minutes.</li>
    <li>Veins are the target — superficial, puncture-tolerant, refillable; arteries are avoided, capillaries are last resort and routine in newborns.</li>
    <li>Preference order: median cubital, cephalic, basilic, hand — and the site is found by palpation, not sight.</li>
    <li>Never draw from the mastectomy-side arm, IV sites, compromised tissue, or fistulas and grafts.</li>
    <li>Serum versus plasma differ by exactly the clotting proteins; the tube additives select the sample each test needs.</li>
    <li>The order of draw prevents invisible additive carryover; label at the bedside, in the patient's presence.</li>
    <li>Two failed sticks means stop and call a colleague, and haemolysis — the leading cause of rejected specimens — is a redraw, not a result.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The map extends in two directions from here: in practice, the failure modes — haemolysis,
    failed draws, the two-attempt rule — and the special populations whose veins and volumes
    reshape every step; and in the discipline, the pre-analytical chain you have just learned is
    recognised as the largest source of laboratory error, which is why certification exams test
    it as hard as the stick itself and why every new collection technology still lands on this
    same map.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The failure modes are the expertise.</strong> Hemolysis — red cells ruptured
    by rough handling, small-gauge needles or violent inversion — spills potassium and
    enzymes into the plasma and is the leading cause of rejected specimens; a haemolysed
    sample is a redraw, not a result. Failed draws ("no blood") trace to a short list:
    needle bevel against the vein wall, a vein that rolled, a tube whose vacuum is spent,
    an angle too shallow or too deep. And the two-attempt rule — after two failed sticks,
    stop and call a colleague — is a patient-safety rule dressed as a humility rule: the
    third attempt is where confidence stops predicting success.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Trust is part of the technique.</strong> Patients faint, flinch and fear
    needles — an estimated substantial share of adults have significant needle fear — and
    the phlebotomist's training includes positioning for fainting (lying down, legs
    raised), honest distraction, and reading the patient whose vasovagal story is about
    to happen before it happens. Paediatric and geriatric patients each reshape the
    procedure: heelsticks and fingersticks for the smallest, small-volume Butterfly
    draws for fragile veins and easy bruising. The profession's measure of quality is
    quietly ambitious: a correct specimen, first stick, that the patient barely noticed.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The map extends into the laboratory's future without losing its anatomy.</strong>
    Pre-analytical variation (tourniquet time, tube fill, mixing, transport temperature)
    is recognised as the largest source of laboratory error, which is why the CLSI
    venipuncture standard GP41 governs the whole procedure and why phlebotomy's
    certification exams (the ASCP BOC PBT among them) test the pre-analytical chain as
    hard as the stick itself. New collection technologies — small-volume tubes, capillary
    microsampling, devices that reduce needle exposure — keep arriving, but every one of
    them still lands on the same map this unit draws: know the vessels, know the blood,
    know the sites to refuse — and identify the patient before anything else happens.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ASCP BOC PBT content outline; CLSI GP41 venipuncture standard; Ernst, <em>Applied Phlebotomy</em>. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
